  "summary": "Richmond, CA City Council is scheduled to vote on a $12 million contract with Axon Enterprise to replace its Flock Safety license plate readers, joining other Bay Area cities moving away from Flock amid data-sharing and privacy concerns. The council will also hear an EFF presentation on whether LPRs have reduced crime.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In response to backlash over data-sharing practices and privacy concerns, Richmond may join a growing list of Bay Area cities saying goodbye to Flock Safety \u2014 and replacing the technology with other vendors.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Axon Enterprise was shortlisted because it proposed a comprehensive, turnkey law enforcement solution that would integrate with technology already utilized by the Richmond Police Department",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Richmond Police Department said it will hold data from the cameras for up to 30 days and won't share it without authorization.",
      "paragraph_idx": 20
    }
  ],
